1. Modules

We can categorise this effect into smoler sub-systems or sub-circuits to get
better understanding of how this thing works.

I will be naming this modules:
1.2 - power supply and power filtering
1.3 - ATMega interfaces
1.4 - FV1 circuit
1.5 - input stage
1.6 - output stage
1.7 - analog distortion
1.8 - analog audio filtering
1.9 - MIDI connection
1.10 - pedal circuit

1.1 Opening thoughts

This modules are really different in size and functionality ranging from small
analog additions to maximize user experience within limited capabilities of this system
and budget, to core aspects like atmega interfaces and FV1 DSP circuit.

Effect is optimised for MIDI automation mainly, it means that its design to use it as live performance
processor to program entire concert within your DAW and send it to device in real time. You can adjust presets
and parameters not only in digital domain, all analog effects like distortion, leveling and EQ will be manageable
through digital interfaces, so you can adjust everything form Oled GUI or MIDI with old-school sounding distortion
for example.

Although I am trying to solve and add stuff sometimes there are things that I cant simply resolve.
Best example is instant cut when we switch effects. Its major problem when you are switching
from long delays or reverbs to other effects. You can try to overcome this problem with MIDI automation
in your DAW but I cannot guarantee you will be happy whit it. Its FV1 design that gets in the way of such things
because suddenly this processor cannot be program as it is but rather it reads programs from eeprom.
We can use another FV-1 that is not synchronized with our first FV and then plug them both to analog mixer circuit
so firs FV-1 can change and second will resolve long delay/reverb without the cut but it requires special algorithms
to calculate when to cut input to second FV and how long we need to resolve effect.

1.2 Power supply and power filtering

System is powered by 9V charger to match industry standard. I suggest to invest some money into it
because with poorly made charger our audio will not be pure, I'm trying to overcome this with use
of different filters but its safe to assume that with better quality charger your audio will be better.
Noise will not only be from your power supply tho, it could be made with poor design decisions.

We should:
-separate the grounds to avoid ground loops.
-make sure that our stabilizers are 5V for atmega and its interfaces and 3.3V for FV1 circuit
and that they are working correctly.
-filter using electrolytic and ceramic capacitors Oled and atmega since they are noise sources,
we should do that as close as possible to them.
-since we using pwm to adjust parameters we need strong filters and dividers to connect to FV1.

1.3 ATMega interfaces



1.4 FV1 circuit
There are many alternatives that are better then this chip but within this price range nothing can compete.
24bit adcs and dacs alone cost more then this processor. This is nowhere near to flexibility of STM32 or
other MCU capable of DSP and that is drawback that cant be ignored as i mention earlier that cut off thing
but we cannot deny high audio quality. Documentation is simple to understand with clear typical application schematic
that after certain modification I used.




